Eric Rosenbaum: Describe the idea of what your character.

2231
03:12:08.600 --> 03:12:08.970
Tim Sackton: Yeah.

2232
03:12:08.970 --> 03:12:09.930
Eric Rosenbaum: do.

2233
03:12:09.930 --> 03:12:10.430
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): It's a…

2234
03:12:10.430 --> 03:12:11.050
Eric Rosenbaum: And that's…

2235
03:12:11.050 --> 03:12:11.570
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Yep.

2236
03:12:11.570 --> 03:12:13.689
Eric Rosenbaum: Tim often has us do that, I think. Yeah.

2237
03:12:13.690 --> 03:12:22.999
Tim Sackton: I will say one thing. I think the main problem with D&D skills is that we as a group roll them way too infrequently.

2238
03:12:23.500 --> 03:12:41.270
Tim Sackton: Skills work much better if it's not… and I'm guilty of this, and I'm… my goal in the next campaign is to do better at this. Tending to be like a, okay, you're spending 20 minutes on this conversation, and then you get one persuasion check, and that's… then you're done.

2239
03:12:41.450 --> 03:12:50.749
Tim Sackton: Right? It's, like, not a really fun way to use skills, but if skills are more… if skills actually steer the… somehow steer the encounter.

2240
03:12:50.890 --> 03:12:52.390
Tim Sackton: Right? Then…

2241
03:12:52.830 --> 03:12:58.869
Tim Sackton: The outcome of each role matters a little bit, and it changes things, but there's not, like, one…

2242
03:13:00.700 --> 03:13:11.280
Mike Sackton: you want to avoid the skill being a success. Skills don't work well as success or failures for anything that matters, is, I think, another way of putting it, right? You want it to be, like.

2243
03:13:12.110 --> 03:13:21.149
Mike Sackton: You said two things, and you make a persuasion check, and that tells you how well they respond to it, but that doesn't necessarily mean you get what you want.

2244
03:13:21.150 --> 03:13:38.260
David S. Kong: Right, right. Well, I guess, like, like, Schwartz, one of the things that I thought was kind of, like, going back to this whole thing about, like, all the skills work, like, at the end of the day, it felt like the most important thing for our characters in your campaign was actually, like, related to this, like, the characters' stories and backstories and their own.

2245
03:13:38.260 --> 03:13:38.650
Eric Rosenbaum: Yeah.

2246
03:13:38.650 --> 03:13:56.369
David S. Kong: you know, it was not at all about skills, or like, you know, like, what you were good at, or what weapons you had. Like, none of that was really important at all. It was like… and that was actually really, I thought, really strong. It was, like, E-Jerk had this big moral thing, that drove the plot, Plato was going through this, like, existential crisis, and then, you know, like…

2247
03:13:56.370 --> 03:14:00.499
David S. Kong: It was… it was a completely, like, story-driven, character-driven.

2248
03:14:00.500 --> 03:14:04.029
David S. Kong: plot, and not at all about, really, skills or dicebreaking.

2249
03:14:04.030 --> 03:14:04.649
Mike Sackton: I will say the…

2250
03:14:04.700 --> 03:14:06.879
David S. Kong: Cool, like, it was really worked.

2251
03:14:06.880 --> 03:14:12.049
Mike Sackton: The mechanic in this session kind of sucked. I mean, it… It was fun, because…

2252
03:14:12.310 --> 03:14:30.799
Mike Sackton: it… of the way it worked out, but the actual mechanic, if you think about it, with a target number on a D20 over 5 people, actually sucks, because the variability is way, way, way too high compared to your pluses, right? So there's no… like, if you're like, oh, you're gonna get… you have to get 50 to win.

2253
03:14:30.910 --> 03:14:39.979
Mike Sackton: It's like, with only one role, it's actually very, very, very difficult to… Have that mechanic.

2254
03:14:40.200 --> 03:14:58.249
Mike Sackton: work well, and the problem with… it's the same trap Tim falls into. It's the same situation that we had in Dunmark, where it's fun, because you have an opportunity to tell a cool story and listen to everybody else's cool stories, and it's very enjoyable from a, like, human interaction

2255
03:14:58.700 --> 03:15:00.859
Mike Sackton: Role-playing narrative standpoint.

2256
03:15:01.040 --> 03:15:04.689
Mike Sackton: Right? But actually, from a gameplay standpoint, it's like…

2257
03:15:05.050 --> 03:15:11.299
Mike Sackton: Rolling one die across a bunch of people and having a buoyant success or failure is not actually a good game.

2258
03:15:11.620 --> 03:15:14.739
Mike Sackton: But it's a second game, even though it's a fun narrative.

2259
03:15:14.940 --> 03:15:15.690
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): This is true.

2260
03:15:15.690 --> 03:15:16.189
Eric Rosenbaum: If you want.

2261
03:15:16.990 --> 03:15:17.520
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Also.

2262
03:15:17.520 --> 03:15:19.760
Eric Rosenbaum: More, like, iteration, more continuity.

2263
03:15:19.760 --> 03:15:21.990
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): You know, a target number for that?

2264
03:15:21.990 --> 03:15:23.229
Mike Sackton: No, of course not.

2265
03:15:23.230 --> 03:15:24.200
Tim Sackton: But it was…

2266
03:15:24.200 --> 03:15:24.780
Eric Rosenbaum: Crazy, didn't.

2267
03:15:24.780 --> 03:15:26.440
David S. Kong: There's no scenario where we weren't gonna get it.

2268
03:15:26.440 --> 03:15:26.920
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): the exchange.

2269
03:15:26.920 --> 03:15:27.320
Tim Sackton: Yeah.

2270
03:15:27.320 --> 03:15:35.680
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): I felt like if the… if the invitation was just, hey, tell a story, that's nice, and then you win, like, that… that doesn't… Yeah. Even though that is fun.

2271
03:15:35.680 --> 03:15:38.930
Tim Sackton: What it is, so don't sugarcoat it, just… just…

2272
03:15:39.220 --> 03:15:43.030
Tim Sackton: I don't think it's that satisfying to…

2273
03:15:43.810 --> 03:15:49.180
Tim Sackton: I mean, this was Mike's point about the… in Dunbar, when you were getting… when you were getting through the…

2274
03:15:50.450 --> 03:15:54.009
Tim Sackton: Monks, and everybody was, like, doing their…

2275
03:15:54.140 --> 03:15:59.010
Tim Sackton: Things is, like, don't make it a check if you're not gonna let it fail.

2276
03:16:00.220 --> 03:16:08.889
Tim Sackton: And you're not gonna let it fail, because, like, it wouldn't be totally unsatisfying for it to fail. So, like, what is… what is the die roll accomplishing there?

2277
03:16:10.330 --> 03:16:12.319
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): Yeah. That's a good question.

2278
03:16:12.320 --> 03:16:18.049
Mike Sackton: I mean, I think there's ways, I don't know what the mechanic is, there probably are ways to make the die roll

2279
03:16:18.860 --> 03:16:26.459
Mike Sackton: actually accomplish something. I just… it… because it was actually fun to use rage and to have a sort of.

2280
03:16:26.460 --> 03:16:28.270
David S. Kong: That was cool. That was really cool.

2281
03:16:28.270 --> 03:16:33.289
Mike Sackton: I have an incentive to make the story fit with my character, right? Exactly.

2282
03:16:33.290 --> 03:16:36.670
David S. Kong: That was great. Yeah, Mike, that was really fucking cool when you did that.

2283
03:16:36.670 --> 03:16:42.140
Mike Sackton: So, like, if you're not rolling at all, it's like, you kind of take… But…

2284
03:16:42.990 --> 03:16:45.749
Mike Sackton: I mean, I think we all kind of knew there wasn't a…

2285
03:16:46.330 --> 03:16:49.840
Mike Sackton: there's a little bit of suspension this week, we know that there's not…

2286
03:16:49.840 --> 03:16:52.649
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): The key thing… the key thing that that mechanic accomplished

2287
03:16:52.790 --> 03:16:59.709
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): was helping the DM feel more like he was running a D&D game, and was using the rules and the dice and things.

2288
03:17:00.480 --> 03:17:01.020
Eric Rosenbaum: That's…

2289
03:17:02.170 --> 03:17:04.079
Mike Sackton: I do think there is…

2290
03:17:04.340 --> 03:17:12.950
Mike Sackton: and I haven't hit on it yet, but I do think there is a mechanic that would be… it's an interesting space to experiment, because we all like this kind of narrative skill.

2291
03:17:14.190 --> 03:17:19.970
Mike Sackton: In both, in a lot of games, it's fun to have this idea of a narrative skill interaction.

2292
03:17:20.130 --> 03:17:24.300
Mike Sackton: But it's always very unclear how to make dive rolling part of it meaningful.

2293
03:17:24.300 --> 03:17:27.100
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): I… I would love it to… Go, go ahead, sorry.

2294
03:17:27.100 --> 03:17:30.139
Eric Rosenbaum: We were just joking about the entity being bloodied.

2295
03:17:30.270 --> 03:17:39.180
Eric Rosenbaum: Yeah. You could actually… And make that several levels of detail, of description of, like, what the… Consequences are.

2296
03:17:39.180 --> 03:17:47.040
Mike Sackton: Well, I think combat works because it's very iterative, and you make many rolls, and so… no combat earlier.

2297
03:17:47.220 --> 03:17:55.360
Mike Sackton: Right? So, combat doesn't swing at a single roll. It's not like… it's not like you're resolving the combat with one D20 roll, and you either kill the monster or die.

2298
03:17:55.860 --> 03:17:56.660
Mike Sackton: Right.

2299
03:17:56.800 --> 03:18:03.869
Mike Sackton: And so, you can respond. There's options. There's, oh man, we're not convincing them, let's change tax. There's… so…

2300
03:18:03.870 --> 03:18:04.330
Eric Rosenbaum: Yeah.

2301
03:18:04.460 --> 03:18:06.489
Mike Sackton: a… a… Right.

2302
03:18:06.750 --> 03:18:08.499
Mike Sackton: But combat also takes an hour.

2303
03:18:08.500 --> 03:18:12.779
Tim Sackton: So the one time I did this, that I'm not sure if Kong picked up on.

2304
03:18:13.240 --> 03:18:18.880
Tim Sackton: was in the Kenzo solo, and it was, like, there was this… there was, like, the chains of…

2305
03:18:18.880 --> 03:18:19.350
David S. Kong: Yes.

2306
03:18:19.350 --> 03:18:20.360
Tim Sackton: of,

2307
03:18:20.360 --> 03:18:21.719
David S. Kong: Yes, I remember that, yeah.

2308
03:18:22.200 --> 03:18:23.959
Tim Sackton: And there was one…

2309
03:18:25.050 --> 03:18:38.250
Tim Sackton: person you were trying to free, who you had… who's… who's, like… I forget what their fear was, but you had convinced yourself that what they wanted was the totally opposite of what they actually wanted. Yeah. And…

2310
03:18:39.300 --> 03:18:43.490
Tim Sackton: And the problem I ran into was it… the role kept failing.

2311
03:18:44.040 --> 03:18:53.359
Tim Sackton: But there was no way to, like, recover, right? There was no way to, like, figure out what you were doing wrong. And I think eventually you just did something generic, and I was like, okay, that's fine.

2312
03:18:53.490 --> 03:18:54.050
David S. Kong: Hmm.

2313
03:18:54.050 --> 03:18:57.329
Tim Sackton: Combat is obvious, like, you can tell, you take damage.

2314
03:18:57.490 --> 03:19:01.979
Tim Sackton: And I don't think D&D works… I don't think it makes sense to have a…

2315
03:19:04.880 --> 03:19:10.950
Tim Sackton: combat mechanic for this sort of thing, for these narrative things. But there needs to be…

2316
03:19:11.420 --> 03:19:17.450
Tim Sackton: And we try and do it, and I know, I mean, I try and do it, and Schwartz was doing it, right, where the thing responds to what you say.

2317
03:19:17.830 --> 03:19:18.420
Eric Rosenbaum: Yeah.

2318
03:19:18.420 --> 03:19:20.820
Tim Sackton: But I think… I think…

2319
03:19:22.720 --> 03:19:29.510
Tim Sackton: I think the way to make it a little bit more iterative and satisfying is a mechanic that actually feels

2320
03:19:29.670 --> 03:19:35.080
Tim Sackton: A little bit more like there's a real chance of failure, or at least a real chance of consequences, where

2321
03:19:35.790 --> 03:19:42.159
Tim Sackton: You know, you need to… reset and do something different is to actually…

2322
03:19:44.890 --> 03:19:47.220
Tim Sackton: It's tricky, because you don't want to have, like.

2323
03:19:48.260 --> 03:19:53.100
Tim Sackton: everybody doesn't want to tell 10 stories, right? It just takes too much time. But have some way that the…

2324
03:19:53.660 --> 03:19:54.530
Tim Sackton: you know.

2325
03:19:54.750 --> 03:20:07.889
Tim Sackton: as you start talking, maybe once it's sort of clear what the story is about, there's some way you can make a rule to see how it's responding. And one of the pieces of advice I've read is the way to think about these rules is not

2326
03:20:10.330 --> 03:20:25.249
Tim Sackton: you don't have an opinion about what the NPC thinks in advance. You roll a die, and that tells you what the NPC thinks, right? And so then you can have it be where there are some, like.

2327
03:20:26.220 --> 03:20:33.480
Tim Sackton: In this scenario, it was like, all of the stories were… Equally appealing to the entity.

2328
03:20:34.150 --> 03:20:39.329
Tim Sackton: That doesn't have to be the case, and there's a way that you can have the die roll

2329
03:20:40.080 --> 03:20:51.590
Tim Sackton: Have a… have multiple dive roles during the course of one story. That gives you a chance to actually have an early failure that you can still recover from, because your story doesn't resonate.

2330
03:20:53.850 --> 03:20:56.089
Tim Sackton: Right? Or something along those lines, right?

2331
03:20:56.090 --> 03:21:02.770
Eric Rosenbaum: I feel like the Hall of Stories was a little bit like that, because we didn't know how it worked at first, so we had to kind of figure it out and try different things.

2332
03:21:03.890 --> 03:21:07.949
Tim Sackton: Well, the… the… but… so, the thing about the Harlem story is, was…

2333
03:21:08.330 --> 03:21:20.440
Tim Sackton: the stakes of any one role were way lower, because what you were trying to do was, like, navigate through the stories to learn information. And so if you… when you failed, it was fine, like…

2334
03:21:21.240 --> 03:21:23.990
Tim Sackton: The… the fundamental problem is… is…

2335
03:21:25.670 --> 03:21:30.120
Tim Sackton: Failure being too unsatisfying to be a real option.

2336
03:21:30.810 --> 03:21:35.139
Tim Sackton: that kind of… Creates lots of mechanical problems, right?

2337
03:21:35.570 --> 03:21:39.280
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): I wonder a little bit about, like, What… what would it…

2338
03:21:39.660 --> 03:21:41.440
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): It seems to me that often.

2339
03:21:41.920 --> 03:21:45.759
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): The die roll determines success or failure, and then, like, that's the…

2340
03:21:45.860 --> 03:21:49.640
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): end of the thing. This is another variety of some of what you were just saying.

2341
03:21:50.000 --> 03:21:51.470
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): But, like, I would…

2342
03:21:51.800 --> 03:21:58.019
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): I wonder how it would flow if the die roll goes, and then the DM asks the player, like.

2343
03:21:58.350 --> 03:22:00.780
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): That's a success. How do you succeed?

2344
03:22:01.050 --> 03:22:05.660
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): What… what did you say that convinced them? Why did you.

2345
03:22:05.660 --> 03:22:07.880
Eric Rosenbaum: I want to do it the opposite way. They're not respectful.

2346
03:22:07.880 --> 03:22:08.990
Tim Sackton: Oh, so…

2347
03:22:08.990 --> 03:22:12.690
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): player is participating, and why… What the…

2348
03:22:13.240 --> 03:22:15.609
Tim Sackton: I don't… Go ahead, Mike.

2349
03:22:15.610 --> 03:22:21.380
Mike Sackton: I was just gonna say, that can be fun, but it doesn't change, like, in this particular scenario. It wouldn't…

2350
03:22:23.240 --> 03:22:26.980
Mike Sackton: Like, there needed to be… To make the die roll meaningful.

2351
03:22:27.260 --> 03:22:31.020
Mike Sackton: It would have had to have been the case that there was a recovery mechanism, right?

2352
03:22:31.020 --> 03:22:31.960
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): about this scenario, yeah.

2353
03:22:31.960 --> 03:22:32.840
Mike Sackton: Right. I think the…

2354
03:22:32.840 --> 03:22:33.200
Tim Sackton: I don't…

2355
03:22:33.200 --> 03:22:33.750
Mike Sackton: beat.

2356
03:22:35.240 --> 03:22:40.659
Tim Sackton: I don't love that in general, because I feel like it's better for the die to reflect

2357
03:22:42.040 --> 03:22:45.190
Tim Sackton: How the world reacts to what you do, and not…

2358
03:22:46.190 --> 03:23:03.670
Tim Sackton: like, thinking about, like, you don't want to be a bard who rolls low and then has to be like, oh, I told a bad story, right? And you also can't control the NPC, so you don't want to be a bard who says, I told a great story, and they didn't like it for some reason.

2359
03:23:03.670 --> 03:23:04.030
Mike Sackton: Whoa.

2360
03:23:04.030 --> 03:23:05.100
Tim Sackton: waking up, right?

2361
03:23:05.100 --> 03:23:07.219
Mike Sackton: I actually disagree with that. I feel like…

2362
03:23:07.400 --> 03:23:11.259
Mike Sackton: if I was, like, thinking about dealt with in high persuasion.

2363
03:23:11.370 --> 03:23:19.969
Mike Sackton: I would have actually probably had a fair bit of fun with, like, you being like, why aren't you persuasive? I could be like, well, you know, the shadow armor just was too much.

2364
03:23:20.080 --> 03:23:21.839
Mike Sackton: Right? And it, you know… Right.

2365
03:23:21.840 --> 03:23:22.230
Eric Rosenbaum: Right.

2366
03:23:22.240 --> 03:23:28.210
Mike Sackton: There's actually… it gives you a… opportunity to…

2367
03:23:29.160 --> 03:23:32.510
Mike Sackton: Controlled and to, to sort of,

2368
03:23:32.950 --> 03:23:38.070
Eric Rosenbaum: I feel like I did that a lot with Seeker, like, different interesting ways that he messed things up.

2369
03:23:38.070 --> 03:23:38.599
Mike Sackton: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

2370
03:23:38.890 --> 03:23:39.480
Tim Sackton: Right.

2371
03:23:39.480 --> 03:23:42.119
Mike Sackton: He took that out sometime in Europe, right?

2372
03:23:42.120 --> 03:23:44.530
Tim Sackton: I think it's… Yes, I think…

2373
03:23:44.920 --> 03:23:53.239
Tim Sackton: I'm not saying it's never good, I'm just saying I think that Generally speaking, it's useful for…

2374
03:23:53.860 --> 03:23:59.590
Tim Sackton: If you think of a die roll as reflecting how the world reacts to what the player said, it allows

2375
03:23:59.890 --> 03:24:05.650
Tim Sackton: the DM to, like, improvise more richly about NPCs, because…

2376
03:24:06.450 --> 03:24:10.080
Tim Sackton: Like, maybe there's an NPC, like, I don't know.

2377
03:24:10.400 --> 03:24:15.069
Tim Sackton: Early Kenz, though, didn't like elves, right? Like, you know, or didn't like shadow elves.

2378
03:24:15.070 --> 03:24:15.880
Rev. David Schwartz (he/him): early.

2379
03:24:15.880 --> 03:24:16.870
David S. Kong: Curly.

2380
03:24:16.870 --> 03:24:18.310
Tim Sackton: Nobody can stop it.

2381
03:24:18.310 --> 03:24:20.259
David S. Kong: I'll FaceTime Continuum capability.

2382
03:24:20.600 --> 03:24:24.850
Tim Sackton: Right? So… so maybe there's an NPC that just doesn't like Al, right?

2383
03:24:25.390 --> 03:24:33.329
Mike Sackton: But I don't necessarily think that… I mean, to your point that you like it when other people make things up, it's… there's a real possibility that that is…

2384
03:24:34.080 --> 03:24:37.919
Mike Sackton: Something that can be collaboratively decided. Does it always have to be up to the…

2385
03:24:38.090 --> 03:24:46.900
Tim Sackton: Yeah, yeah, no, no, no, I'm not… no, I'm not disagreeing at all, I'm just saying that I don't think it's an all-one, all the other situation, and…

2386
03:24:47.100 --> 03:24:51.879
Mike Sackton: to me, the challenges are both… and I really feel bad, but…

2387
03:24:51.880 --> 03:24:52.440
David S. Kong: Yeah.

2388
03:24:52.440 --> 03:24:54.739
Mike Sackton: I'm gonna leave you with two thoughts, and then I'm gonna go to bed.

2389
03:24:54.990 --> 03:25:00.980
Mike Sackton: I think the challenge is that when… Stakes are meaningful.

2390
03:25:01.810 --> 03:25:11.980
Mike Sackton: one and done is never satisfying, unless it's gonna be an automatic success, because otherwise it's too random, right? There's too much randomness in a single D20 roll.

2391
03:25:11.980 --> 03:25:13.509
David S. Kong: Yeah, right. Yeah, yeah.

2392
03:25:13.510 --> 03:25:19.350
Mike Sackton: And the other bit is that it feels kind of unsatisfying to…

2393
03:25:20.360 --> 03:25:29.120
Mike Sackton: there's not enough reflection of skill in… the D20 role is so variable compared to stats and pluses.

2394
03:25:29.260 --> 03:25:32.580
Mike Sackton: That you get into this scenario where

2395
03:25:32.800 --> 03:25:44.509
Mike Sackton: being the person who is good at persuasion doesn't really matter that much. On many… in combat, it's not like that, because you make many, many rolls. So on average, it comes out. But if you're only making.

2396
03:25:44.510 --> 03:25:45.170
Eric Rosenbaum: Yeah.

2397
03:25:45.170 --> 03:25:52.049
Mike Sackton: when making a small number of rolls, the D20 variability is so high compared to your pluses that you don't feel like.

2398
03:25:52.050 --> 03:25:52.460
Eric Rosenbaum: Interesting.

2399
03:25:52.460 --> 03:25:54.140
Mike Sackton: better, right? And so…

2400
03:25:54.140 --> 03:26:00.540
Tim Sackton: This is… this is my whole point. We should be making 5 times as many skill rolls a session. I think it would be more fun.

2401
03:26:00.540 --> 03:26:13.569
Mike Sackton: Yeah, or whatever it is, or whether it's… the failure is different depending on how good you are, or what… there's other options than just… but it's more satisfying to…

2402
03:26:14.290 --> 03:26:18.360
Mike Sackton: like, that was one thing that sort of annoyed me about Delaware, was, like.

2403
03:26:18.550 --> 03:26:22.120
Mike Sackton: Even with a plus 15 on persuasion.

